WEBVTT

00:00.280 --> 00:02.600
- [Instructor] We are
told the polynomial p of x

00:02.600 --> 00:05.120
is equal to four x to the
third plus 19 x squared

00:05.120 --> 00:10.120
plus 19 x minus six has a
known factor of x plus two.

00:10.510 --> 00:15.150
Rewrite p of x as a
product of linear factors.

00:15.150 --> 00:18.430
So pause this video and see
if you can have a go at that.

00:18.430 --> 00:20.430
All right, now let's
work through it together.

00:20.430 --> 00:23.080
So if they didn't give
us this second piece

00:23.080 --> 00:25.270
of information that as a known factor of x

00:25.270 --> 00:29.930
plus two, this polynomial
would not be so easy to factor.

00:29.930 --> 00:31.890
But because we know we have a known factor

00:31.890 --> 00:33.940
of x plus two, I could divide that

00:33.940 --> 00:36.690
into our expression and
figure out what I have

00:36.690 --> 00:39.450
left over, and then see if
I can factor from there.

00:39.450 --> 00:40.570
So let's do that.

00:40.570 --> 00:45.570
Let's divide x plus two
into our polynomial.

00:46.710 --> 00:50.150
So it's four x to the third power plus 19

00:50.150 --> 00:54.370
x squared plus 19 x minus six.

00:54.370 --> 00:57.090
And we've done this
multiple times already.

00:57.090 --> 00:58.550
We look at the highest-degree terms.

00:58.550 --> 01:00.840
X goes into four x to the third.

01:00.840 --> 01:04.020
Four x squared times, I
put that in the x squared

01:04.020 --> 01:06.210
or the second-degree column.

01:06.210 --> 01:10.040
Four x squared times x
is four x to the third.

01:10.040 --> 01:15.040
Four x squared times two is
eight x, eight x squared.

01:15.240 --> 01:18.140
And then I wanna subtract
these from what I have up here.

01:18.140 --> 01:19.510
So I'll subtract.

01:19.510 --> 01:23.060
And then I'm going to be
left with 19 x squared

01:23.060 --> 01:26.230
minus eight x squared is 11 x squared.

01:26.230 --> 01:31.230
And then I will bring
down, bring down the 19 x.

01:31.530 --> 01:33.880
So plus 19 x.

01:33.880 --> 01:37.320
And so once again, look
at x and 11 x squared.

01:37.320 --> 01:40.830
X goes into 11, x squared 11 x times.

01:40.830 --> 01:43.760
So that's plus 11 x.

01:43.760 --> 01:46.930
11 x times x is 11 x squared.

01:46.930 --> 01:50.100
11 x times two is 22 x.

01:50.100 --> 01:53.380
Need to subtract these from
what we have in that teal color.

01:53.380 --> 01:57.270
And we are left with 19
minus 22 of something

01:57.270 --> 01:58.710
is negative three of that something.

01:58.710 --> 02:00.730
In this case, it's negative three x.

02:00.730 --> 02:05.200
And then we bring down that negative six.

02:05.200 --> 02:08.140
And then we look at, once again, at the x

02:08.140 --> 02:09.650
and the negative three x.

02:09.650 --> 02:13.460
X goes into negative three
x negative three times.

02:13.460 --> 02:17.710
And so negative three times
x is negative three x.

02:17.710 --> 02:20.220
Negative three times two is negative six.

02:20.220 --> 02:22.290
And then if we wanna
subtract what we have in red

02:22.290 --> 02:23.960
from what we have in magenta.

02:23.960 --> 02:26.170
So I could just multiply
them both by negative.

02:26.170 --> 02:29.750
And so everything just cancels
out and we have no remainder.

02:29.750 --> 02:32.680
And so we can rewrite p of x now.

02:32.680 --> 02:36.230
We can rewrite p of x as being equal to x

02:36.230 --> 02:40.080
plus two times all of this business,

02:40.080 --> 02:45.080
four x squared plus 11 x minus three.

02:45.180 --> 02:46.730
Now we're not done yet because we haven't

02:46.730 --> 02:49.310
expressed it as a product
of linear factors.

02:49.310 --> 02:52.210
This one over here is
linear but four x squared

02:52.210 --> 02:54.360
plus 11 x minus three,
that's still quadratic.

02:54.360 --> 02:56.740
So we have to factor that further.

02:56.740 --> 02:58.580
And let's see, there's a couple of ways

02:58.580 --> 02:59.413
we could approach it.

02:59.413 --> 03:01.270
We could use, well, we could try with this

03:01.270 --> 03:05.210
and the quadratic formula or
we could factor by grouping.

03:05.210 --> 03:07.190
And to factor by grouping,
and the whole reason

03:07.190 --> 03:08.700
we have to factor by grouping is we have

03:08.700 --> 03:11.430
a leading coefficient
here that is not one.

03:11.430 --> 03:14.020
And so we need to think of two numbers

03:14.020 --> 03:17.690
whose product is equal to
four times negative three.

03:17.690 --> 03:19.100
So we have to think of
two numbers, let's just

03:19.100 --> 03:19.933
call them a and b.

03:19.933 --> 03:23.180
A times b needs to be equal to four times

03:23.180 --> 03:25.150
negative three, which is negative 12.

03:25.150 --> 03:29.510
And a plus b needs to be equal to 11.

03:29.510 --> 03:32.630
And so the best that I
can think of, they have

03:32.630 --> 03:34.610
to be opposite signs 'cause their product

03:34.610 --> 03:35.780
is a negative.

03:35.780 --> 03:39.710
So If I had negative, if
I had, if I had positive

03:39.710 --> 03:41.450
12 and negative one, that works.

03:41.450 --> 03:44.760
If a is equal to negative
one and b is equal

03:44.760 --> 03:46.780
to positive 12, that works.

03:46.780 --> 03:49.210
And so what I wanna do is I wanna take

03:49.210 --> 03:52.450
this first-degree term
right over here, 11 x,

03:52.450 --> 03:56.250
and I wanna split it into a
12 x and a negative one x.

03:56.250 --> 03:57.600
So let's do that.

03:57.600 --> 04:01.615
So I can, let's just focus on this part,

04:01.615 --> 04:04.320
on this part right now
and then I'll put it all

04:04.320 --> 04:05.790
back together at the end.

04:05.790 --> 04:10.790
So I can rewrite all of this
business as four x squared.

04:12.600 --> 04:15.460
And instead of writing the 11 x there,

04:15.460 --> 04:17.990
I'm gonna use this blue
color, I'm gonna break

04:17.990 --> 04:19.810
it up as a 12 x.

04:19.810 --> 04:24.810
So plus 12 x and then minus one x.

04:24.930 --> 04:27.230
Notice these two still add up to 11 x.

04:27.230 --> 04:31.760
And then I have my minus three.

04:31.760 --> 04:34.750
And then, let's see,
out of these first two

04:34.750 --> 04:36.470
what can I factor out?

04:36.470 --> 04:38.810
Let's see, I can factor out a four x.

04:38.810 --> 04:41.070
So I can rewrite these first two as,

04:41.070 --> 04:43.500
and if this is unfamiliar
to you I encourage you

04:43.500 --> 04:46.240
to review factoring by
grouping on Khan Academy.

04:46.240 --> 04:48.890
So if we factor out a
four x that's going to be,

04:48.890 --> 04:51.870
it's going to be, we're
gonna be left with an x here.

04:51.870 --> 04:55.260
And then we're gonna be
left with a three over here.

04:55.260 --> 04:59.900
And then these second
two terms, if we factor

04:59.900 --> 05:02.820
out a negative one, so
I'll write negative one,

05:02.820 --> 05:06.300
times, we're going to
have an x plus three.

05:06.300 --> 05:09.950
And so then we can factor
out the x plus three.

05:09.950 --> 05:11.950
So let's do that.

05:11.950 --> 05:13.420
I'm running out of colors.

05:13.420 --> 05:16.980
So I factor out the x
plus three and I am left

05:16.980 --> 05:21.980
with x plus three times, times four x.

05:24.020 --> 05:25.370
And I'm gonna keep these colors the same

05:25.370 --> 05:27.110
so you know where I got 'em from.

05:27.110 --> 05:29.330
Four x minus one.

05:29.330 --> 05:32.200
This is a very colorful
solution that we have over here.

05:32.200 --> 05:33.060
And there you have it.

05:33.060 --> 05:36.240
I factored the second part
into these two factors.

05:36.240 --> 05:38.520
And so now I can put it all together.

05:38.520 --> 05:42.410
I can rewrite p of x as a
product of linear factors.

05:42.410 --> 05:47.410
P of x is equal to x plus
two times x plus three

05:48.440 --> 05:50.700
times four x minus one.

05:50.700 --> 05:52.410
Four x minus one.

05:52.410 --> 05:56.143
And we are done.
